  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🕯️ Dark Ambient Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for music made of cold rooms, long tones and things in the cellar."
    },
    "intro": "Good dark ambient names set the scene before a note is heard: a Latin word, a buried place, a numbered ward. Dark ambient is usually one person and a lot of reverb, and the name does much of the work. This **dark ambient name generator** makes project, collaboration and album names for dark ambient, drone and horror ambient.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good dark ambient names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator, across the three styles:</p><ul><li><strong>Glacies</strong>: Latin for ice, and a single cold word, the dark ambient way.</li><li><strong>The Weeping Mine</strong>: a place underground, where the sound seems to come from.</li><li><strong>Cities Under Snow</strong>: a whole landscape in three words.</li><li><strong>Bells of the Grey Hour</strong>: a drone project named after one long, slow sound.</li><li><strong>Long Tones for Car Parks at Dawn</strong>: a drone album that tells you exactly what to expect, and where to listen.</li><li><strong>The Night Nurse</strong>: a horror ambient project named after a figure, not a monster.</li><li><strong>Annexe 19</strong>: a numbered room, which is somehow worse than a named one.</li><li><strong>Hymns for Damp Walls</strong>: horror ambient album titles work best when they're quiet and specific.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How dark ambient names itself",
        "content": "<p>Dark ambient grew up in the 1980s and 90s alongside industrial music, with artists like Lustmord turning drones, field recordings and deep reverb into something closer to a place than a song. Drone goes back further, to the long, held tones of composers like La Monte Young, and Brian Eno's <em>Music for Airports</em> (1978) set the pattern for ambient album titles that name a space.</p><ul><li><strong>Dark ambient</strong> names are cold and buried: Latin words, frozen places, catacombs. <em>Crypta</em>, <em>Hiems</em>, <em>Inside the Permafrost</em>.</li><li><strong>Drone</strong> names are calmer and very British here: estuaries, reservoirs, radio masts and long tones. <em>Mills of the North Sea</em>, <em>Music for Reservoirs</em>.</li><li><strong>Horror ambient</strong> names sound like a found tape: a house and a year, a hospital ward, someone's job. <em>Wetherby House, 1938</em>, <em>The Sexton</em>, <em>The Whitlock Tapes</em>.</li></ul><p>Collaborations join two projects with an ampersand, the way split releases do: <em>The Projectionist &amp; the Lamplighter</em>.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Using a dark ambient name",
